Scientists have long warned of more intense extreme weather events in the decades to come due to our fast-warming climate. The question they are asking is how we are going to be better prepared as we become more prone to natural disasters.A group of researchers in Switzerland believe that artificial intelligence modeling could revolutionise forecasting systems.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/08ea2e16-ae8b-11f1-a14f-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260912-101151-101341-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/08ea2e16-ae8b-11f1-a14f-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260912-101151-101341-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0dd2d310-ae8b-11f1-9b83-2fe206701493</guid><pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 09:46:00 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>French police suspect Normandy train derailment was result of sabotage</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20260912-french-police-investigate-suspected-sabotage-in-train-derailment</link><description>
